  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nNoticeably cooler air has settled into the Treasure State in the \nwake of a cold front that pushed through overnight. Temperatures \nas of early afternoon have generally struggled to reach the low to\nmid 60s thanks to this cooler air, which has also conveniently \ncleared out the smoke for most of the area, though some smoke does\nlinger across Beaverhead, Madison, and Gallatin Counties this \nafternoon. Afternoon fair weather clouds have developed in this \ncooler air mass, but do not expect to see any precipitation from \nthese clouds aside from a very isolated and short lived light rain\nshower. \n\nThis cooldown will be rather short lived, however, as mid to \nupper level westerly flow returns on Tuesday, which will allow \ntemperatures to moderate back up to the still relatively pleasant \nmid to upper 70s for most population centers. As upper level \nridging gradually builds back into the area, expect temperatures \nto rise here at the surface, with highs reaching the 90s for the \nlower elevations by Thursday. As temperatures rise, humidity will \nfall, with RH values likely to fall into the teens each afternoon.\n\n\nUpper level ridging looks to break down late this week, which \nwould result in some increased winds as it does so. Should this \nhappen, critical fire weather conditions will be possible thanks \nto the continued dry air and increased winds. However, at this \ntime there remains a lot of uncertainty as to just how this will \nplay out. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFire Weather Friday/Saturday: the main area of impactful \nuncertainty in the forecast at this time appears to be the \npotential for near critical to critical fire weather conditions \nFriday and/or Saturday. As the upper level ridge breaks down, a \nsmall jet streak looks to move overhead, which could have \ndifferent effects depending on the size, strength, and exact path \nthe jet streak takes. Most models at the moment seem to put the \njet streak somewhere over Montana Friday, though the \nlocation/timing varies, with some, including the GFS, putting the \njet streak in a way that would result in extremely low RH (in the \nsingle digits), and very deep thermal mixing that would bring down\nsome 20-30 mph wind gusts. Other solutions would still likely be \ngusty, but would potentially not be as extremely dry. In any case,\nexpect elevated fire weather conditions later this week, but just\nhow elevated remains uncertain at this time. -Ludwig",
